**Criteria answer: NO — MXFS is not production ready.** 29 defects remain open on the RULE-6 ledger; the marker stays unwritten.

**What this session established — the P248 verification ran, and the fix is 94% effective with the remaining gap root-caused to proven code paths:**

1. **Verification run #2 executed** (teardown on 0.11.454, the actual measurement): **30 of 32 nodes leaked nothing** — the sess151 release_all retire path is verified working at scale. Pre-fix, every teardown leaked on most nodes; post-fix, the only leaks were **one entry each on test16 and test23: the root inode (id=128) with a single PR tenure**, both via the owed path. test29's apparent -1 delta was a dmesg ring wrap, and a timeline trap (sess151's run #1 actually ran at 06:27Z, not 05:40Z) was resolved from transcript timestamps — the baseline and delta are sound.

2. **Root cause of the residual leak is proven, not guessed** (RULE 4): `stop()` sets `running=false` before release_all; `caw_slot()` refuses retries when `!running`, so a one-shot SCSI UNIT ATTENTION (guaranteed occasionally during a 32-node teardown storm — `key=0x6` visible in-window) makes the first CAS attempt terminal (`cas_rc=-108`). The slot goes owed; the teardown drain clears the slot bits successfully, but its completion path (`caw_owed_release → lreq_gc`) never retires the entry's tenure, and `lreq_gc` refuses to free any entry with live tenure. Every field of the leaked-entry dump matches this chain.

3. **Fix design is ready for the mandatory RULE-5 consult** (this is cycle 2 on the defect): (A) retire tenure on teardown-drain obligation completion — safe because `ops_closed + release_all_done` means no publication can land; (B) optionally allow bounded CAS retries during the teardown pass so one-shot UAs don't fail confirm-clear at all. Verification needs ≥3 prep_cluster cycles (~96 node-teardowns) given the ~2/32-per-cycle hit rate.

4. **Host note (not MXFS):** clyde has two ext4 `mount` processes stuck in D-state since Aug 5 (jbd2 superblock write never completing) that wedge `ps`/`pgrep` — 522 processes piled up behind them. Per RULE 2 I did not touch host recovery; a manual look at that ext4 device is the user's call. Workaround for sessions: scan `/proc/*/stat` directly.

Everything is recorded in ccmemory note `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess152-p248-owed-path-leak-ROOT-PROVEN-fix-design-ready`. **Next session:** GPT consult on fix A(+B), implement per ruling as 0.11.455, deploy, 3-cycle verification census — then the overdue memory compaction (task #2) and the next ledger defect.

